mkfifo - make a name for a pipe

SYNOPSIS
    mkfifo NAME...

DESCRIPTION
    Two programs started separately cannot share an ordinary pipe. A pipe
    is found by inheritance - fork hands both ends down - and two programs
    a shell started have no common ancestor to inherit from. A FIFO is the
    same pipe with a name on the filesystem, so both can find it.

        mkfifo /tmp/f
        cat /tmp/f &
        echo hello > /tmp/f

    Opening one waits for the other end. That is what makes it a
    rendezvous rather than a file: without it the echo would finish before
    the cat arrived, and what it wrote would go nowhere.

    The file on disk holds nothing and never will. It exists so the pipe
    has a name, an owner and permissions - which is what a filesystem is
    for. Everything that moves goes through the kernel.

    ls shows one with a trailing | , and a p in the first column of a long
    listing.

BUGS
    A writer that opens before any reader is let through when the reader
    arrives, but what it writes does not reach that reader. Open the
    reading end first - `cat fifo &` then `echo x > fifo`, which is the
    order a shell produces anyway - and it behaves.

SEE ALSO
    sh, ln, ls
